# The front proxy.
#
# Three jobs, and each one is something the kernel cannot do for itself:
#
# 1. Serve /static. The kernel rate-limits every GET at 100 a minute per IP with
#    no way to configure it, and static assets fall in that bucket, so a visitor
#    reading ten pages has their fonts throttled. Measured: the 101st request for
#    a stylesheet returns 429. Serving the site's own files here takes them off
#    that budget entirely. Anything the site does not carry falls through to the
#    kernel, which is where the image's own css and js live.
#
# 2. Set Cache-Control on HTML. The kernel sets it on static files and on nothing
#    else, so an HTML page arrives with no caching policy at all and every cache
#    between here and the reader invents one.
#
# 3. Terminate TLS and serve /sitemap.xml, which the kernel serves with relative
#    <loc> values that the sitemap protocol does not permit. Both are configured
#    in the site block below.
#
# How the site is served lives in shared.caddy, imported below. This file is the
# local entry point: one address, no certificate, no redirect. The production
# entry point is Caddyfile.production, which imports the same snippet and adds
# the hostname, the certificate and the www redirect.

{
	admin off
	# Local runs have no public DNS, so certificate provisioning is off unless the
	# production profile turns it on by giving SITE_ADDRESS a real hostname.
	auto_https {$AUTO_HTTPS:off}
}

import /etc/caddy/shared.caddy

{$SITE_ADDRESS} {
	import site
}
